<Article slug="best-monitor-arms-singapore">
<p>A monitor arm is one of the cheapest ergonomic upgrades you can make. It lifts your screen to eye level, frees up desk space, and makes it easy to share a screen or switch between sitting and standing. Here are three options sold in Singapore, from a S$38 budget arm to Ergotron's well-known LX series.</p>

<h2>Quick picks</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th>Arm</th><th>Monitor weight</th><th>Screen size</th><th>Price at time of writing (28 Oct 2026)</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>North Bayou F80</td><td>2 to 9 kg</td><td>17" to 30"</td><td>around S$38</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ergotron LX</td><td>3.2 to 11.3 kg</td><td>up to 34"</td><td>around S$174.80</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ergotron LX Pro</td><td>1.8 to 10 kg</td><td>up to 34"</td><td>around S$211.80</td></tr>
</tbody></table>

<Product id="north-bayou-f80" rank={1} verdict="Best budget pick: a gas-spring arm for under S$40.">
<p>The North Bayou F80 is a popular low-cost gas-spring arm. Harvey Norman Singapore listed it at around S$38 (down from S$45) on 28 October 2026, with a 12-month manufacturer's warranty. Published specs:</p>
<ul><li>Fits 17" to 30" displays weighing 2 to 9 kg</li><li>VESA 75×75 and 100×100</li><li>About 26 cm of vertical lift, ±85° tilt, 180° swivel, 360° rotation</li><li>Built-in cable management</li></ul>
<p><strong>Watch out for:</strong> check your monitor's weight (without its stand) is inside 2 to 9 kg. The arm is meant for small and mid-size monitors, not heavy ultrawides.</p>
</Product>

<Product id="ergotron-lx" rank={2} verdict="Best overall: the long-standing benchmark arm.">
<p>The Ergotron LX is one of the most widely recommended monitor arms. Singapore IT retailer SourceIT listed the matte black and white versions at around S$174.80 on 28 October 2026. According to Ergotron's spec sheet, it has:</p>
<ul><li>Support for screens up to 34" weighing 3.2 to 11.3 kg</li><li>33 cm of lift and 75° of tilt</li><li>360° pan and rotation</li><li>VESA 75×75 and 100×100</li></ul>
<p><strong>Watch out for:</strong> it has a minimum weight of 3.2 kg, so a very light portable monitor may not stay in place.</p>
</Product>

<Product id="ergotron-lx-pro" rank={3} verdict="Best for lighter or deeper setups: the updated LX.">
<p>The LX Pro is Ergotron's newer take on the LX. Singapore retailer Langya Tech listed it at around S$211.80 on 28 October 2026. According to Ergotron's spec sheet, it has:</p>
<ul><li>Support for one monitor up to 34" weighing 1.8 to 10 kg</li><li>33 cm of lift and 85° of tilt</li><li>180° monitor pan and 360° rotation</li><li>VESA 75×75 and 100×100</li><li>A standard clamp that fits desk edges about 1 to 6 cm thick</li></ul>
<p><strong>Watch out for:</strong> Ergotron notes that very deep monitors can reduce the usable weight capacity, so check the spec sheet if you have a deep or curved screen.</p>
</Product>

<h2>Before you buy: 4 checks</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>VESA mount.</strong> Look for four screw holes on the back of your monitor, usually 75×75 or 100×100 mm. Without them you'll need an adaptor.</li>
<li><strong>Weight without the stand.</strong> Look up your monitor's weight "without stand" on its spec sheet and make sure it sits inside the arm's range.</li>
<li><strong>Desk edge.</strong> Clamps need a solid edge of the right thickness, with clearance underneath. Glass and thin particleboard tops may need a reinforcement plate.</li>
<li><strong>Standing desks.</strong> An arm adds weight and leverage at the back edge, so check your desk's load rating.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>For a single mid-size monitor on a budget, the <strong>North Bayou F80</strong> at about S$38 is hard to argue with. If you want a longer-lasting arm with more range and more published detail, the <strong>Ergotron LX</strong> is the benchmark, and the <strong>LX Pro</strong> suits lighter monitors.</p>
</Article>
